<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Default (release) network policy: no cleartext HTTP, ever.

  This replaces a blanket android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" on <application>, which applied to
  published builds too and meant any http:// request — an accidental URL, a redirect off https, a
  downgraded tile fetch — went out in the clear with nothing to stop it.

  DEFAULT_BACKEND_ROOT is https://pet.uzoukwu.net (Cloudflare tunnel), so the default configuration
  is unaffected. What this does cost: a user who repoints the app at a plain-http LAN or tailnet
  backend in Settings will find it fails in a release build. That is deliberate — it follows the
  same line REPORTS_ENABLED already draws in BackendConfig.kt, where a capability that is fine on a
  trusted network has no business being reachable in a published build. Use a debug build for
  http backends; app/src/debug/res/xml/ overrides this file to allow them.

  Note that this cannot be scoped to "whatever host the user typed": a network security config is a
  static resource parsed at process start, and there is no runtime API to add permitted domains. So
  the choice really is all-or-nothing per variant, which is why it splits debug/release.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
</network-security-config>
